Tournament Analysis over unit-distance-to-tournament gauges
rank | gauge | outscore | note
--- | --- | --- | ---
1 | unit graph traceability | 6 | intrinsic yes/no unit-spine predicate
2 | frontier-gain recursion | 5 | state-local construction/deletion ledger
3 | direction-pair quotient | 4 | retains Moser shell channels but forgets point order
4 | spine-order flip gauge | 3 | chooses tie path from a found unit spine
5 | proof-obligation tournament | 2 | vertices are cores, ears, directions, obstructions
6 | lexicographic point flip | 1 | useful diagnostic, strongly order-dependent
7 | raw pair-distance tournament | 0 | pretty but loses embedding side channels
score histogram: {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1}
directed 3-cycles: 0
SCC sizes: (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)
Hamiltonian path count: 1
Challenged assumption: tournament vertices need not be points.  For proof work the better vertices can be unit directions, dense cores, frontier ears, traceability obligations, and totally-unfaithful filters.

Interpretation
- The intrinsic question is graph traceability of the unit-distance graph.
- A traceable unit graph lets either flip convention carry an all-unit mandatory path after choosing the tie order from the spine.
- A fixed external order, such as lexicographic coordinates, can obscure that spine; any apparent flip/flop there is a gauge artifact.
- A genuine flop would require an extremal unit-distance graph with no unit Hamiltonian path, likely forced by three or more essential boundary ears or separator branches.
